P0172 Changes were confined to `/work/tmp/review`: tests were appended to `quinn-proto/src/tests/mod.rs`, `connection/ack_frequency.rs`, and `congestion/bbr/bw_estimation.rs`. Each original file remains a verbatim prefix of its scratch counterpart; no production fixes were made.

P0173 Sparse connection-ID rotation advances into an empty slot. quinn-proto/src/cid_queue.rs:92. CidQueue::next selects the second occupied entry with iter().nth(1), but then uses its position as a ring-slot displacement when updating cursor and offset. If the active CID has sequence 0 and the next supplied CIDs have sequences 2 and 4, the missing sequence 1 makes the rotation advance by one instead of two. The active slot is then empty and active() panics, interrupting an otherwise valid CID rotation after reordered delivery.

P0174 From that scratch workspace, `cargo test --locked --offline -p quinn-proto review_ -- --nocapture --test-threads=1` executed **15 tests: 14 failed as described above and one control passed**. The MTU case also contains a passing control. Detailed output is retained in [regressions.log](/work/tmp/regressions.log). ACK-frequency and BBR reproductions are component tests; the other cases exercise simulated connections or public helpers.

P0175 Switching to unordered reads exposes duplicate buffered stream bytes. quinn-proto/src/connection/assembler.rs:40. Assembler::ensure_ordering(false) switches the receive mode without normalizing frames already buffered in ordered mode. If an application reads offsets 0..3 in order, buffers two copies of 3..6 and an overlapping 4..8 frame, then switches to unordered reads, the existing heap entries remain independently readable. Offsets 3..6 can consequently be delivered more than once, even though unordered stream reads must still deliver each stream byte once. The received-range tracking only prevents duplicates from later insertions.

